File Sync
=========

The module synchronizes Brainbow tag trees with a filesystem directory.
Configure the base directory in the Tryton server configuration::

    [file_sync]
    path = /srv/tryton/files

Root tags marked ``Synchronize`` become directories below this path.
Brainbow documents are UTF-8 Markdown files and tagged attachments are binary
files.  Synchronization metadata uses SHA-256 digests to detect independent
changes and preserve both versions when a conflict occurs.

Document and attachment changes are processed by Tryton's default queue.
Enable workers on every Tryton server that creates these tasks::

    [queue]
    worker = True

Run the worker only on a server that has access to ``[file_sync] path``::

    trytond-worker -c trytond.conf -d database

``trytond-file-sync.py`` does not consume this queue.  It only watches changes
made on the filesystem, so both processes are required for bidirectional
synchronization when ``[queue] worker`` is enabled.

The executable ``trytond-file-sync.py`` watches the path recursively with
``watchdog``, using the native filesystem observer (inotify on Linux).  It is
available directly in the module source directory and is also installed as an
executable by ``setup.py``.  It accepts the standard Tryton daemon options and
requires exactly one database.  With ``-vv``, it logs every filesystem
creation, modification or deletion that it synchronizes and the resulting
ERP/filesystem operations::

    trytond-file-sync.py -vv -c trytond.conf -d database

If the native observer can not be initialized because the process or system
has exhausted its file descriptors or inotify watches, the process logs the
error and switches to watchdog's polling observer with a 30-second interval.
Syncthing metadata such as ``.stfolder``, ``.stignore`` and ``.stversions`` is
excluded from both filesystem events and full synchronization.
